1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,900
Okay, welcome to the chapter on skin pathology. This is the last chapter in the text. It is a relatively lower yield topic. I do recognize that, especially for the purposes of board examinations. However, I would like you to realize that within this quote-unquote low yield topic, there are some high yields that you do need to be aware of.

2
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:39,260
And so really, the purpose of our discussion will be to focus on those high yields. Before we begin, however, we should remind ourselves that the skin is a barrier. And of course, it's a barrier against the outside world and the environmental insults that are present in the outside world. And it's also a barrier against the loss of fluid. And so these are some of the primary functions in the skin.

3
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:48,800
Now, before you can understand the disorders that we're going to discuss, you do need to know some of the normal histology of the skin. So let's remind ourselves that this is the basement membrane.

4
00:00:48,800 --> 00:01:03,580
The basement membrane separates the epidermis from the underlying dermis. Now, the key cell that makes up the epidermis is the keratinocyte. And the keratinocytes within the epidermis are actually organized in multiple layers.

5
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:14,680
And these layers all have names and they have certain characteristics. So the first layer and the layer that's up against the basement membrane is called the stratum fasalis, also known as the basal layer.

6
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:25,180
And the most important thing for the purposes of exams is that the basal layer is the stem cell layer. It is the layer that regenerates new epidermis. And that's particularly high yield.

7
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:38,280
The layer above that is called the stratum spinosum, also called the spinal layer. And the reason it's called the stratum spinosum is that these cells present in this layer are actually connected by these things called desmosomes.

8
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:48,280
So you have desmosomes which tightly connect the cells together. So again, desmosome in the cytoplasm, another desmosome in the cytoplasm, and that sort of then tightly connects these cells together.

9
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:58,380
Now, when the cells get slightly separated apart, you can actually see little strands of cytoplasm between the cells, which represent these bridges between the cells.

10
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:14,380
And basically what they're highlighting is the fact that underlying those connections are desmosomes. And so because you can see these little connections or lines between the cells, they look like spines or little spinous processes of the cytoplasm.

11
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:21,380
So we call that the stratum spinosum. And again, the highlight of the stratum spinosum is that you've got desmosomes between the keratinocytes.

12
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:33,380
Now, as you go up a little further, you begin to see little granules within the cytoplasm. And so they call that the stratum granulosum. And that's because you've got granules in the keratinocytes.

13
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:43,380
And of course, the very top of the skin would have keratin in anucleate cells, and that's called a stratum corneum. And so again, these are the few layers to be aware of.

14
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:54,380
Now, also, you've got to remember that underlying the epidermis is the dermis. And the dermis contains connective tissue, blood vessels, and that's particularly high yield.

15
00:02:54,380 --> 00:03:04,380
The blood vessels come up into the dermis. Remember that there are no blood vessels in the epidermis. They're all in the dermis. And also within the dermis, you've got these adnexal structures.

16
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:11,380
For example, the hair shafts are in the dermis, the sweat glands are in the dermis, the sebaceous glands are in the dermis, etc.

17
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:17,380
And so these are some of the basic principles to be aware of. Now, I will review this as we go along and talk about individual diseases,

18
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:22,380
but I wanted to plant this basic understanding in your mind before we move forward.

19
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:29,380
Now, let's review this using histology. So this would be the dermis. This would be the epidermis made up of keratinocytes.

20
00:03:29,380 --> 00:03:37,380
And of course, they're separated by a basement membrane. Now, remember that the layer of cells that's right up against the basement membrane is the basal layer,

21
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:44,380
or the stratum basalis. And then above that, you've got the spinal layer. Now, can you look here and notice that there's a connection,

22
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:54,380
these little lines in between the cells, and that represents the spinous processes. Of course, that's because you've got desmosomes connecting the cytoplasm here and here.

23
00:03:54,380 --> 00:04:00,380
And so you're seeing these little connections as the cells get pulled apart a little bit. So again, this would be the spinal layer.

24
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:09,380
Above that, you can see that there are these little purple granules within the cells. And so this is this granular layer or the stratum granulosum.

25
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:16,380
And then finally, you've got this pink keratin present in anucleate cells. And again, that's called the stratum corneum.

26
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:23,380
The first category of disorders that we're going to discuss are the inflammatory dermatoses. And the first of these is atopic dermatitis.

27
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:30,380
The dermatitis implies that we've got inflammation of the skin. And basically, this results in a pyritic, i.e. itchy,

28
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:38,380
erythematous, oozing rash, with vesicles and edema. This is also known as eczema. And when you see a patient with eczema,

29
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:44,380
you know that it looks red, it itches, it's a little bit wet because it's edematous. And if you look very carefully,

30
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:51,380
you can see these tiny vesicles. So that's atopic dermatitis. It classically involves the face and the flexor surfaces.

31
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:58,380
And you often see it in patients with asthma and allergic rhinitis. And these are also atopic types of reactions as well.

32
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:06,380
And so our understanding is that it is also a type I hypersensitivity, just as asthma and allergic rhinitis are also a type I hypersensitivity

33
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:12,380
to environmental antigens. Contact dermatitis is very similar to atopic dermatitis.

34
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:17,380
Again, you have this pyritic erythematous oozing rash with vesicles and edema, so it looks like eczema.

35
00:05:17,380 --> 00:05:26,380
However, it's due to exposure to an allergen. So it's not really associated with asthma and allergic rhinitis, and it's not a type I hypersensitivity.

36
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:34,380
Instead, there's a substance that contacts the skin, hence the term contact, which then results in the inflammatory process.

37
00:05:34,380 --> 00:05:41,380
Now this is classically associated with irritants. And so in contact dermatitis, you ask the question, what's the direct irritant?

38
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:49,380
So for example, it could be poison ivy or nickel that's present in jewelry. Now the example here would be that the patient would purchase a watch,

39
00:05:49,380 --> 00:05:54,380
they place the watch on their wrist, and then they develop a little rash underneath the base of the watch.

40
00:05:54,380 --> 00:06:00,380
And that's because there can be nickel, for example, in the metal that's used to cover the backside of the watch.

41
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:06,380
And so that would result in a type IV hypersensitivity. And also poison ivy is another example of contact dermatitis,

42
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:12,380
and it too is a type IV hypersensitivity. Now irritant chemicals can also do this.

43
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:16,380
For example, patients may use a detergent, and that may result in a contact dermatitis.

44
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:24,380
And then there are some drugs that can also result in a contact dermatitis. And one classic example would be penicillin.

45
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:28,380
Now the treatment of contact dermatitis would be to remove the offending agent.

46
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:33,380
And then you could use topical steroids because that would help to reduce the inflammatory response.

47
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:39,380
And topical steroids could be used both in contact dermatitis and also in atopic dermatitis.

48
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:44,380
The next disorder is acne vulgaris. We're all very well aware of acne.

49
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:48,380
It's extremely common, especially in adolescents. And it has a range of lesions.

50
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:52,380
For example, you can develop comedones, which are whiteheads and blackheads.

51
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:55,380
You can develop pustules, which we classically call as pimples.

52
00:06:55,380 --> 00:07:01,380
And you can get severe scarring of those pimples, which would then result in nodules, especially in severe acne.

53
00:07:01,380 --> 00:07:07,380
Now acne is actually due to chronic inflammation of the hair follicles and the sebaceous glands.

54
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:11,380
The way by which this occurs is that if this is the dermal-epidermal junction,

55
00:07:11,380 --> 00:07:15,380
remember that within the dermis you've got hair shafts and hair follicles.

56
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:19,380
Associated with those hair shafts and hair follicles are sebaceous glands.

57
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:21,380
So let's label that with S.

58
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:25,380
Now what happens in adolescents, especially with the excess hormone related to adolescents,

59
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:30,380
is that you can get excess keratinization of the hair shaft.

60
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:33,380
Now, of course, the hair shaft is lined by keratinocytes.

61
00:07:33,380 --> 00:07:36,380
And so you can get excess production of keratin by the hair shafts.

62
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:40,380
And at the same time, these sebaceous glands are responsive to hormones.

63
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:45,380
And so you can get excess production of sebum from these sebaceous glands.

64
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:52,380
Now with this block and excess production, what's going to happen is you're going to develop a collection of sebum,

65
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:55,380
which would then be called a whitehead or a blackhead.

66
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:58,380
Now, of course, this is a tube and you've blocked the tube.

67
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:02,380
And general principle in pathology that whenever you block a tube, you're going to increase your risk for infection.

68
00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:06,380
So the infection that will come along is P. acnes.

69
00:08:06,380 --> 00:08:09,380
And that's very high yield for the purposes of board exam.

70
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:10,380
Again, P. acnes.

71
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:14,380
And P. acnes infection will then result in production of lipases.

72
00:08:14,380 --> 00:08:17,380
And the lipases will break down the sebum.

73
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:19,380
So the sebum will begin to break down.

74
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:24,380
And now when you break down the sebum, that's going to release pro-inflammatory fatty acids.

75
00:08:24,380 --> 00:08:28,380
So you're going to get inflammation within this whitehead or blackhead.

76
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:30,380
And it's the inflammation that then leads to a pimple.

77
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:33,380
And if that pimple scars, to a nodule.

78
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:36,380
Now let's go back and just read what we say in the text.

79
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:40,380
So it's due to chronic inflammation of hair follicles and sebaceous glands.

80
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:41,380
I've explained that already.

81
00:08:41,380 --> 00:08:44,380
You get a hormone-related increase in sebum, as I explained.

82
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:51,380
And you get excess keratin, which then blocks the hair follicles, which then results in comedones or whiteheads or blackheads.

83
00:08:51,380 --> 00:08:59,380
And then you get a P. acnes, very high yield, P. acnes infection that leads to inflammation resulting in pustules or nodules.

84
00:08:59,380 --> 00:09:01,380
Now the treatment is all based on this understanding.

85
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:06,380
And so, for example, you could use benzoyl peroxide as an antimicrobial agent.

86
00:09:06,380 --> 00:09:08,380
And the goal is to knock out the P. acnes.

87
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:11,380
And when you knock out the P. acnes, you wouldn't get the inflammation.

88
00:09:11,380 --> 00:09:14,380
In addition, you could use vitamin A derivatives.

89
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:17,380
So you know that for more severe acne, we use vitamin A derivatives.

90
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:21,380
And the idea is that the vitamin A derivatives will reduce keratin production.

91
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:27,380
Remember that one of the key functions of vitamin A is to maintain specialized epithelium in the body.

92
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:30,380
And so the hair follicle is one of those specialized epithelium.

93
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:35,380
So you want to make sure that there's good vitamin A so that you don't get that excess keratin production.

94
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:40,380
And so, again, vitamin A derivatives can also be used in the treatment of acne.

95
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:42,380
The next disorder is psoriasis.

96
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:47,380
It's characterized by well-circumscribed salmon-colored plaques with silvery scale,

97
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:51,380
usually arising on the extensor surfaces and the scalp.

98
00:09:51,380 --> 00:09:55,380
It can also involve the nails, and that can result in pitting of the nails.

99
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:57,380
Here's a classic picture of psoriasis.

100
00:09:57,380 --> 00:10:03,380
You can see these well-circumscribed salmon-colored plaques with all of this silvery scale.

101
00:10:03,380 --> 00:10:08,380
And, of course, this is occurring on the surface of the knee, which would be an extensor surface.

102
00:10:08,380 --> 00:10:12,380
Psoriasis is due to excessive keratinocyte proliferation.

103
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:15,380
We don't know what causes it, but it's possibly autoimmune.

104
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:23,380
And recall that whenever you have an autoimmune disorder, you're looking for an environmental trigger in a genetically susceptible individual.

105
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:29,380
And so the environmental trigger is thought to be trauma because it classically arises in areas of trauma.

106
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:34,380
And the genetic susceptibility is that there is an association with HLAC.

107
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:38,380
Here's the classic histology from a patient with psoriasis.

108
00:10:38,380 --> 00:10:42,380
And, of course, remember that psoriasis is due to excessive keratinocyte proliferation.

109
00:10:42,380 --> 00:10:48,380
So, one key finding will be that you'll have hyperplasia with an increased thickness of the epidermis.

110
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:50,380
And we call that acanthosis.

111
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:56,380
The second finding will be that you'll have all this excessive keratin on the top of the epidermis.

112
00:10:56,380 --> 00:11:00,380
Now, of course, that's what results in the silvery scale that you see clinically.

113
00:11:00,380 --> 00:11:05,380
And, in addition, notice that there are nuclei, all these blue dots within that keratin.

114
00:11:05,380 --> 00:11:12,380
And so we call that parakeratosis, which is hyperkeratosis, excess keratin, with retention of nuclei.

115
00:11:12,380 --> 00:11:15,380
So, again, you'll see parakeratosis, which explains the silvery scale.

116
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:21,380
Now, if I showed you a little higher power, you would see collections of neutrophils within this stratum corneum.

117
00:11:21,380 --> 00:11:25,380
And those collections of neutrophils are called Monroe microabscesses.

118
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:30,380
And, finally, you'll get elongation of the dermal papillae.

119
00:11:30,380 --> 00:11:35,380
Now, notice that these are the dermal papillae here and here, and they are elongated.

120
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:41,380
In addition, the epidermis above the elongated papillae is thin, and that's particularly important.

121
00:11:41,380 --> 00:11:42,380
Now, why is that so important?

122
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:48,380
Because if you were to pull away the silvery scale above these dermal papillae,

123
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:53,380
because this epidermis is so thin, you would actually get exposure of the blood vessels.

124
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:56,380
Remember, the blood vessels normally run in the dermal papillae.

125
00:11:56,380 --> 00:12:02,380
So you would get exposure of the blood vessels with a pinpoint bleed above the area that you remove the silvery scale.

126
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:05,380
And that's very high yield. That's called auspitz sign.

127
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:12,380
I.e., when you take the silvery scale and peel it away, you'll see pinpoint bleeds underneath the silvery scale.

128
00:12:12,380 --> 00:12:18,380
And that's because you've exposed the tiny blood vessels running within the dermal papillae.

129
00:12:18,380 --> 00:12:21,380
The treatment of psoriasis is based on two principles.

130
00:12:21,380 --> 00:12:27,380
The first principle is our understanding that it's likely autoimmune, and so if it's likely autoimmune,

131
00:12:27,380 --> 00:12:32,380
corticosteroids and immune modulating therapy would help to reduce the severity of the disease.

132
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:37,380
In addition, because we know that it's due to excessive keratinocyte proliferation,

133
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:43,380
we could try to damage the keratinocytes as they're proliferating so that they can't create the disease.

134
00:12:43,380 --> 00:12:48,380
And one way to do that would be to hit them with UV light, in particular UVA light.

135
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:53,380
Now when you hit them with UVA light, that will then disrupt the cells from proliferating,

136
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:57,380
because it will damage the cells, and so therefore you'd reduce the severity of the disease.

137
00:12:57,380 --> 00:13:02,380
Now one thing you can do to increase the absorption of the UVA light is to give a drug called sorolin,

138
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:06,380
and this entire combination is called PUVA, P-U-V-A.

139
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:09,380
Again, UVA plus sorolin.

140
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:12,380
And so these are the hallmarks of treating psoriasis.

141
00:13:12,380 --> 00:13:15,380
The last disorder in this section is lichen planus.

142
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:18,380
This is the one that's associated with all the P's.

143
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:25,380
It's pruritic or itchy, planar in that it's flat on the surface, polygonal in shape, purple or violet in color,

144
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:27,380
and it's a raised papule.

145
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:32,380
And so again, these are the P's that are associated with lichen planus.

146
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:37,380
It commonly involves the wrists, the elbows, and the oral mucosa.

147
00:13:37,380 --> 00:13:43,380
And particularly when it involves the oral mucosa, you can often see these reticular white lines on the surface,

148
00:13:43,380 --> 00:13:45,380
which are called Wickham striae.

149
00:13:45,380 --> 00:13:46,380
Here's a picture of lichen planus.

150
00:13:46,380 --> 00:13:48,380
Notice that it's got some of these P's.

151
00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:50,380
It's polygonal in shape.

152
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:51,380
It's purple.

153
00:13:51,380 --> 00:13:52,380
It's a papule.

154
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:55,380
It would be planar or flat on its surface.

155
00:13:55,380 --> 00:13:59,380
And so again, these are the classic P's associated with lichen planus.

156
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:02,380
Here's the classic histology of lichen planus.

157
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:06,380
And the key finding is that you get inflammation at the dermal-epidermal junction.

158
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:07,380
Here's the epidermis.

159
00:14:07,380 --> 00:14:08,380
Here's the dermis.

160
00:14:08,380 --> 00:14:14,380
And notice that you've got all these little blue inflammatory cells present at the dermal-epidermal junction.

161
00:14:14,380 --> 00:14:17,380
Now when you get damage to the dermal-epidermal junction,

162
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:22,380
then one of the things that happens is you get a sawtooth appearance to the dermal-epidermal junction.

163
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:28,380
And that's particularly high yield, a sawtooth appearance at the dermal-epidermal junction.

164
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:30,380
The etiology of lichen planus is unknown.

165
00:14:30,380 --> 00:14:35,380
However, particularly high yield, it is associated with chronic hepatitis C virus.

166
00:14:35,380 --> 00:14:37,380
And that concludes this section.

